# HTTPS front end for SirisAI, self-signed (tls internal) since this stays
# LAN-only -- browsers only expose microphone recording
# (navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia) on a secure context (https://, or
# localhost), and SirisAI's own plain-http address doesn't qualify. The
# first visit from any browser/device shows a security warning (Caddy's
# root CA isn't in any device's trust store) -- click through once
# ("Advanced" -> "Proceed"); most browsers remember that per origin.
# Everything else about SirisAI is unchanged: this only adds a second,
# optional way to reach it.
# A hostless "*:8443" address can't pre-issue a cert (Caddy needs to know
# which name(s) to put in it), so list the actual names this is reachable
# as -- the LAN IP and localhost. Caddy still binds 0.0.0.0:8443 regardless
# of these hostnames; they're only used for cert issuance/SNI matching.
#
# default_sni matters because most devices on the LAN will reach this via
# the bare IP, and RFC 6066 forbids sending the SNI extension for an IP
# literal -- curl, browsers, etc. all connect with no SNI at all in that
# case (only tools like `openssl s_client -servername <ip>` fake it, which
# is why that looked fine in testing while curl/browsers didn't). Without
# this, an SNI-less handshake falls through to Caddy's broken hostless
# fallback and the TLS handshake fails outright.
{
	default_sni 192.168.0.100
}

192.168.0.100:8443, localhost:8443 {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy sirisai:8000
}
